  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDry weather is expected to continue through Friday and Saturday\nas surface high pressure remains in control. Temperatures \nremain a a bit above climatology through Friday. Southerly flow \nand rising heights behind the departing high may push daytime \nhighs some 10 degrees above climatology on Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe upper level pattern heading into the Sunday through Monday\nnight time frame continues to support a broad upper ridge over\nthe east with Hurricane Isaias coming ashore and tracking north\ninto the Ohio Valley and the Lower Great Lakes. The trend in the\nensembles, including the NBM as well as some deterministic\nmodels show a shift to the west of the track of the landfalling\nhurricane. Precip begins to move into the forecast area by late\nSaturday night. By 00Z Monday, the low center begins to track \neast but surface high pressure off the east coast slows the exit\nas bit. This factor will need to be monitored as additional \nresidence time will mean more rainfall, and potential minor \nproblems, especially with the leaves beginning to fall and \nclogging drains in the city. For now, the swath of moisture \nexits to the east. \n\nAt this time, concerning the rainfall, the mean solution has\nabout 1.20\" to 1.40\" for the event. A more amplified pattern\ncould keep the tropical system in place a bit longer. Thus, a\n90th percentile solution brings 2.00\" to 2.50\" to the forecast\narea in a 48 hour period. Most FFG guidance across the area is\naround 1.50\" to 1.75\" for 1 hour and up to 3.00\" for 6 hour\namounts. The NBM probs for exceeding 2.50\" is around 10% So,\nstill at this point, no major flooding is expected but\ncertainly needs watched given the slowed track potential. The\nprobs of exceeding 250 J/Kg of SB CAPE is still around 30% so\nsevere is still not expected to be a concern.",
